Subject: Re: possible pkgsrc emacs framework improvements?
To: Thomas Klausner <wiz@NetBSD.org>

Hi Thomas,

The rest of the sweep finished, so here are the numbers I owed you.
Of the 67 packages that select emacs30nox, 37 build, 2 I did not try
(both mozc-elisp, which want bazel), and 28 fail -- fourteen because
of this change and fourteen for reasons that have nothing to do with
it.  This is on a tree with none of my fixes in it; I had measured it
once before while fixing as I went, and that pass reported four fewer,
which is what fixing and measuring in the same tree does.

The fourteen fall into four shapes, and each takes one line.

Four get a 404 fetching their distfile, because mk/fetch/github.mk has
GITHUB_PROJECT?= ${PKGBASE} and the prefix moves PKGBASE, so the fetch
asks for github.com/ziglang/emacs30nox-zig-mode and WRKSRC points
where the archive does not unpack: zig-mode, lua-mode, emacs-neotree
and markdown-mode.

Two name a renamed package without the prefix and can then never be
satisfied: misc/emacs-wiki asks for semi>=1.14.6, and devel/flim adds
an unprefixed apel>=10.8 to BUILDLINK_API_DEPENDS.apel while apel's
own buildlink3.mk already says ${EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX}apel>=10.  There
is a third that no build here can reach: devel/doxymacs asks for
w3-[0-9]* inside .elif ${EMACS_VERSION_MAJOR} <= 21, and www/w3
becomes emacs20-w3-4.0b47nb4, so the line can never be satisfied --
but only under emacs20 or emacs21, which is why the sweep went
straight past it.  Those three are all there are on trunk; I matched
on the name boundary rather than as a substring, which first gave me
xemacs-packages for emacs-packages and w3m for w3.

Three have a PLIST that spells share/emacs/site-lisp itself while the
install follows ${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}, so the files go to the version
directory and the file check reports them missing from where the PLIST
says: twittering-mode, emacs-dict-client and rsltc.

Five spell ${PKGBASE} on one side of the install and the bare name on
the other, which agree only while the prefix is empty.  Three are in
the lisp directory: editors/matlab-mode creates
${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE:C|^xemacs-||} and installs into
${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE}, textproc/xslide installs into
${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE} while its PLIST says
${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/xslide, and www/emacs-w3m sets WRKSRC to
${WRKDIR}/${PKGBASE}-${GITHUB_TAG}.  Two are in share/doc:
editors/gnuserv lists share/doc/${PKGBASE} in INSTALLATION_DIRS while
doc-install writes share/doc/gnuserv, so that name becomes a file
holding whichever README was installed last rather than a directory,
and textproc/dictem has the same pair.

There is a fifth shape that does not fail at all, and it is the one I
could not have found by reading.  devel/cqual and
inputmethod/tamago-tsunagi build green and install into
share/emacs/site-lisp rather than into the version directory: their
PLIST and their install both spell the old path, so the two agree and
nothing catches it.  cqual does not spell EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX either,
so it stays cqual-0.991nb21 and writes exactly where it always did.  A
package that quietly ignores the new location seems worse to me than
one that stops, and only the packing list of the built package shows
it:

  cqual-0.991nb21                        share/emacs/site-lisp/cqual/
  emacs30nox-tamago-tsunagi-5.0.7.1nb3   share/emacs/site-lisp/egg/
  the other 35 that built                share/emacs/30.2/site-lisp/

The other fourteen are not about the change: three have lost their
distfile (cobol-mode 402, flyspell and ispell-emacs 404), eight want a
dependency this sandbox cannot build (TeX Live, gcc14, rust), mail/mu
has a PLIST bug of its own that is in the other mail, mail/vm reaches
misc/bbdb2 -- which you have since removed, and vm no longer asks for
it -- and mail/wl-snapshot is stopped by a CONFLICTS line that I think
is stale, also in the other mail.

The sweep tree is a 2 September copy, so I went back and checked each
package I name above against trunk today rather than trusting the
copy: all four GITHUB_PROJECT lines are still absent, all three
unprefixed dependencies are still unprefixed, all six hand-written
lisp paths are still hand-written, and all five ${PKGBASE} pairs are
still as described.

Going through those lines turned up a handful of packages that are
broken on trunk today, before any of this.  That is not really about
your three points, so it is in a mail of its own rather than buried
here.

Two of the things that had to be fixed were in my own modules.mk, and
only building found them.  EMACSLOADPATH for the build still named the
old shared site-lisp, so anything byte-compiling against another elisp
package stopped with "Cannot open load file" -- devel/flim could not
require apel's install.el.  Nothing in the checks I had showed this:
EMACS_LISPPREFIX was right, and the installed Emacs finds install.elc
with the same flags the build uses.  Only the environment variable
pointed at the old place.  Separately, the EMACS_VERSION_REQD path
still had the empty-_EMACS_PKGDIR fatal I thought I had fixed, because
I had only fixed it on the EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED side.

A third was in something I had already told you was done.  I wrote
that the choice reads the package's list in the order it was written,
the way pyversion.mk walks PYTHON_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED, and that was the
intent but not the code: the loop that intersects the two lists walked
modules.mk's, so the package's order never reached the answer.
misc/mailcrypt writes emacs20 first and still got emacs31.  The loop
runs the other way now.  Reordering does move the answer, but only
where it should -- with EMACS_TYPE set to something the package does
not take, mailcrypt given "emacs20 emacs29" picks emacs20 and given
"emacs29 emacs20" picks emacs29, while if the default is in the list
the default still wins.  I mention the condition because I first tried
to show this with devel/apel and got the same answer both ways: apel
names emacs20 in EMACS_VERSIONS_INCOMPATIBLE, so only one candidate
survived the filter and there was nothing left to order.

The pbulk part was worse, and I am sorry to have said it was done.  I
wrote that it was three lines in mk/pbulk/pbulk-index.mk of the same
shape as the python and ruby entries.  It was three lines and they did
nothing: the class also has to be named in _PBULK_MULTI, which is the
list the loop walks, so nothing ever read them.  It also wanted the
filtered list rather than the written one, for the reason python takes
_PYTHON_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED -- otherwise a package naming a version
pkgsrc no longer has puts it in the bulk index.  So it is four lines,
and with them devel/apel gives

  PKGNAME=emacs30nox-apel-10.8.0.20201107nb2
  PKGNAME=emacs29-apel-10.8.0.20201107nb2
  ... six in all, emacs29 through emacs31nox

with the default first, while devel/pcl-cvs, which takes emacs20 and
nothing else, gives the single line it should, and devel/gmake, which
never includes modules.mk, is untouched.

I should say where all this was measured, since it is one machine for
most of it: NetBSD 11.0/amd64.  The parts that matter most I have also
run on i386 11.0, in a throwaway VM -- apel, flim, zig-mode, dictem,
gnuserv and emacs-w3m build there with the prefix on their names, the
pbulk enumeration comes out the same, and Emacs finds the lisp under
share/emacs/30.2/site-lisp with share/emacs/site-lisp still beside it.
Nothing else has been near another platform.

mail/mu turned out not to be about placement at all -- it is the one
in the other mail, and its lisp goes exactly where it should.  It is
worth one word here though, because it is the kind of thing your third
point would find on its first pass: nobody builds mu against a nox
Emacs today, so nobody has seen it.

It also answers a question I had about the prefix.  emacs30 and
emacs30-nox11 conflict, and they share share/emacs/30.2/site-lisp, so
it is tempting to give both the emacs30- prefix and let one binary
package serve either -- the dependency can be spelled
{emacs30,emacs30-nox11}>=30.1<31 and pkg_info matches it.  mail/mu says
no: the file list is not the same for the two.  So they are different
packages and the prefix should keep saying which.

One failure is the change refusing, which is what you asked for.
mail/vm in my copy depends on misc/bbdb2, which accepts only emacs21,
and the build stops with

  ERROR: No Emacs that pkgsrc still has is accepted here
  ERROR: Accepted versions are: emacs21 emacs21nox
  ERROR: pkgsrc has: emacs31 emacs31nox ... xemacs214nox

rather than with "Cannot open /version.mk".  It cannot happen on trunk,
though: you took bbdb2 out with emacs21 and mail/vm rev 1.47 no longer
includes its buildlink3.mk.  The same goes for the fifteen packages I
said still ask for emacs21 -- that is my 2 September copy, not pkgsrc;
jde, leim21, bbdb2 and nxml-mode went with the version and nothing on
trunk names it.  So the intersection is worth taking for the next
retirement rather than for a mess sitting there today.

Since my earlier figures came from that copy, I took them again from
trunk, fetching each Makefile and PLIST.  The build results above are
still from the copy, with the current modules.mk dropped in; every
package I name still exists on trunk with the same lines.  87 packages
read modules.mk, leaving out wip and the emacs and xemacs packages
themselves.  86 have a PLIST -- counting PLIST.emacs and the like, not
just PLIST, which is where I lost one the first time.  Of those, 78
write ${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}, six write the path by hand (the same six I
named before) and two install no lisp at all.  Of the 26 that pass a lisp directory into their own
build, all 26 pass the variable.  (I said 25 and named chat/zenicb last
time; that was a miscount -- what I matched there is a commented-out
PLIST_SUBST line, and zenicb passes nothing into a build at all, it
writes ${EMACS_LISPPREFIX} straight into INSTALLATION_DIRS and its
install rule.)

Three of those six need a second line, because the install writes the
shared path as well and changing only the PLIST splits the two:
inputmethod/tamago-tsunagi and devel/cqual, which leave it to a
bundled makefile, and devel/rsltc, whose own Makefile has
${DESTDIR}${PREFIX}/share/emacs/site-lisp/rsl spelled out three times.
All six build here with both lines.  Left alone, tamago-tsunagi and
cqual are the two that build green into the old directory, and the
other four stop -- which side you land on depends only on whether the
PLIST or the install was the one written by hand.

devel/rsltc carries a caveat: it wants devel/gentle, which pkgsrc
marks broken on this platform, so the only reason it got as far as the
packing list here is that I had a gentle installed from the experiment
below.  The mismatch itself is in the files and does not depend on
that.

rsltc took a detour worth mentioning on its own, and it ends somewhere
I did not expect.  It wants devel/gentle, which carries
BROKEN_ON_PLATFORM= ${LP64PLATFORMS}, so I had written it off.  With
that line taken out for the experiment, on NetBSD 11.0/amd64, gentle
builds with nothing but K&R-era warnings, and it really runs: the
distfile ships ast.g and no ast.c, and the log has gentle turning all
eleven grammars into C.  rsltc builds against it and works:

  $ rsltc HELLO.rsl
  Checking HELLO ...
  rsltc completed: 0 error(s) 0 warning(s)
  $ rsltc bad.rsl
  bad.rsl:3:13: syntax error

with the line and column right.  The same thing was then tried from
the same tree on i386, where pkgsrc does not mark gentle broken at
all, and there it builds, installs, and dies on the first grammar:

  gentle ast.g
  gmake: *** [std_make:31: ast.c] Segmentation fault (core dumped)

That first i386 run was on a throwaway NetBSD 9.4 VM, which differs
from my amd64 box in release as well as in word size, so I ran it
again on i386 11.0 and got the same thing, on the same first grammar.
Three points now:

  amd64 11.0   gentle builds, all eleven grammars, rsltc works
  i386  9.4    gentle builds and installs, segfault on ast.g
  i386  11.0   the same

so it is the word size and not the release.  What the line says is the
other way round: it calls LP64 broken, and LP64 is where it worked.  I
have no core to offer -- both i386 runs were throwaway VMs -- and I am
not asking you to change the line on three data points and no
backtrace.  But since i386 is not marked broken, devel/rsltc is
failing there in any bulk build today, and that is also what keeps it
out of the one on amd64.

The name is the expensive half, and most of it is outside modules.mk.
EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX only does anything where the package spells it in
PKGNAME, and 24 of the 87 do not -- 22 last time; I had been reading
only the Makefile and missing the ones that set PKGNAME in options.mk
or not at all.  Adding it moves PKGBASE, and six
variables under mk/ take ${PKGBASE} as their default -- PKGWILDCARD
and PKG_SYSCONFVAR in bsd.pkg.mk, FILEBASE in
pkgformat/pkg/package.mk, and GITHUB_PROJECT, GITLAB_PROJECT and
CODEBERG_PROJECT in mk/fetch -- so anything resting on the default
moves with it.  Ten of the 87 fetch from GitHub without setting
GITHUB_PROJECT; none uses gitlab or codeberg.  Nine spell ${PKGBASE}
themselves -- the nine above.

None of that shows up until a package gains the prefix, so I took one
of the 22 that lack it -- misc/color-theme -- added the PKGNAME line
and built it.  It breaks twice over: MASTER_SITES is
.../releases/${PKGBASE}/, which becomes
.../releases/emacs30nox-color-theme/ and 404s, and the install writes
ELISPDIR=${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/${PKGBASE} while the PLIST says
${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}/color-theme:

  ERROR: .../site-lisp/emacs30nox-color-theme/themes/color-theme.elc

That is one of the 24.  Three of those 24 spell ${PKGBASE}, and they
are the ones to look at first: misc/color-theme above,
mail/mailutils (--with-mh-bindir=${PREFIX}/libexec/${PKGBASE} and
DOCDIR=share/doc/${PKGBASE}) and www/emacs-w3m
(WRKSRC=${WRKDIR}/${PKGBASE}-${GITHUB_TAG}).  The other 21 have no
${PKGBASE} of their own, so for them it may well be the one line --
but I would rather not say so until they have been through a build.

One more, which I found late and have since fixed, but it is worth
showing because it was mine and because of where the answer was.
Making EMACS_TYPE the default rather than a requirement means a
package that does not accept it falls back to one it does accept.  On
its own that is pyversion.mk's behaviour and fine; in a chain it was
not.  www/emacs-w3m accepts emacs20 and devel/apel does not, so with
EMACS_TYPE=emacs20 emacs-w3m asked for emacs20-apel while apel built
itself as emacs29-apel, and nothing said why the dependency never
appeared.

pyversion.mk does not have that problem, and the reason is one I had
walked past: when pkgsrc resolves a dependency it passes the pattern
as PKGNAME_REQD, and pyversion.mk reads the version back out of it, so
the dependency is built for the version its dependent chose.  With the
same six lines here:

  => Full dependency emacs20-apel>=10: NOT found
  => Verifying reinstall for ../../devel/apel
  ERROR: This package does not build with emacs20
  ERROR: Accepted versions are: emacs29 emacs29nox ... emacs31nox

which names the version, the package and what would work.  It is where
the tree stops today too, though with "No valid Emacs version
installed found" and no version named.

One more that is not about placement at all.
EMACS_VERSIONS_INCOMPATIBLE is set by devel/apel, and no line of
modules.mk reads it, while pyversion.mk and rubyversion.mk both
subtract their equivalent from what they will choose from.  Now that
the selection walks the package's own list, leaving it out is one more
condition in the same loop, so I have done that.  Side by side, with
EMACS_TYPE=emacs30nox and devel/apel:

  modules.mk as it is   default emacs30nox, and emacs30nox again when
                        told EMACS_VERSIONS_INCOMPATIBLE=emacs30nox
  with the line read     emacs29 instead

apel's own line names emacs20, which it does not accept anyway, so
nothing moves there -- that line has been doing nothing twice over,
which is presumably why nobody noticed the variable was never read.

I had been saying the lisp goes somewhere Emacs already searches
without ever checking it, so I did.  I installed the ones that build,
listed every .elc they own -- 390 files -- and asked Emacs for each by
the name it would be required under.  All 390 are on load-path:
share/emacs/30.2/site-lisp and its first-level subdirectories, with
share/emacs/site-lisp still there beside them, so a package that has
not moved yet is unaffected.  Requiring the main library of each
package works for 24 of 33.  The nine that fail are worth a word,
because I nearly wrote them up as damage: they fail for reasons inside
the elisp, not for where it lives.  markdown-mode and rmail-mime want
eieio-make-class-predicate, which this Emacs does not have even after
(require 'eieio); verilog-mode wants XEmacs's lucid; gnuserv wants
XEmacs's temp-directory.  I copied three of the nine into the old
shared site-lisp and required them from there, and they fail with the
same messages, character for character.

The lisp moves and the info files do not, and that turns out to matter
more than it sounds.  _EMACS_INFODIR is picked by flavour, not by
version, so every GNU Emacs gets ${PKGINFODIR} and every XEmacs gets
lib/xemacs/site-packages/info.  With the version in the name, two
builds of the same package are two packages that write the same info
file, and pkg_add stops the second:

  pkg_add: flim-1.14.9nb6: conflicting PLIST with
           emacs30nox-flim-1.14.9nb6: info/mime-en.info

I hit that by accident -- I was rebuilding flim against the stock
modules.mk while the emacs30nox one was still installed -- but it is
the same collision emacs29-flim and emacs30-flim would have, and 30 of
the 87 install info files.  XEmacs has
it too: xemacs214 and xemacs215 both give
lib/xemacs/site-packages/info.

So being able to build every version is not yet being able to install
them.  Moving info under the version as well is the obvious answer and
I have not done it, because Emacs finds info through INFOPATH and the
dir file rather than by looking beside the lisp, so it is a change
with a second half I would rather agree with you first.

One cost of the second point that I have not seen raised.  Putting the
prefix in PKGNAME takes the package out of the vulnerability list's
reach, quietly:

  pkg_admin audit-pkg flim-1.14.2             -> reports CAN-2004-0422
  pkg_admin audit-pkg emacs30nox-flim-1.14.2  -> nothing
  pkg_admin audit-pkg xemacs-flim-1.14.2      -> nothing

The third line is the one that matters: this is already true of the
xemacs- packages in pkgsrc today.  An advisory can be written to cover
it, but only by enumerating -- {,emacs30nox-}flim<1.14.3 matches while
*-flim<1.14.3 and emacs*-flim<1.14.3 do not, because the glob does not
combine with a version bound.  That shape is at least not a novelty:
of the 30234 entries in the list 3778 already open with a brace list,
1183 of them with the empty alternative, and w3m{,-img}<0.3.2.2 shows
a brace and a version bound together.  So the cost is the width of the
line rather than a new mechanism -- with the prefix carrying the
flavour only it is {,xemacs-}, and with the version in it every
advisory for an elisp package has to name every Emacs.  Nothing in the
current list is affected: I decompressed it and went through the 92
entries whose name starts with emacs or xemacs, and every one is
bounded below what pkgsrc ships.  The only advisory for an elisp
package rather than for Emacs itself is flim<1.14.3, and pkgsrc has
1.14.9.

CONFLICTS goes the same way, and that one has teeth.
inputmethod/tamago-tsunagi says CONFLICTS= tamago-[0-9]*, which matches
tamago-20020909nb5 and does not match emacs20-tamago-20020909nb5:

  pkg_admin pmatch 'tamago-[0-9]*' tamago-20020909nb5          matches
  pkg_admin pmatch 'tamago-[0-9]*' emacs20-tamago-20020909nb5  does not

Two packages that both select emacs20 would then land in the same
share/emacs/20.7/site-lisp/egg with nothing declared between them.
Spelling the prefix in the pattern fixes it and sharpens it at the
same time -- with ${EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX}tamago-[0-9]* the line matches
emacs20-tamago while tamago-tsunagi is built for emacs20, and does not
match it under emacs30nox, which is right: the two only collide when
they share a lisp directory.  That one is in emacs-framework/pkg-fixes/
on the branch, with the rest of the package changes; say the word and I
will paste whichever of them you want to look at into a mail instead.

I watched the shape of this happen for real, though for a different
reason: editors/gnuserv's CONFLICTS names a pattern that matches no
package, and pkg_add refused xemacs-nox11 over bin/gnuattach instead
of the line refusing it first.  So wherever the prefix moves PKGNAME,
the CONFLICTS lines pointing at that package want looking at.  The
vulnerability list is the same mechanism without a fix I can offer, so
I would rather put that half in front of you than decide it.

Happy to send the framework part on its own first, with the package
fixes following in whatever grouping suits you.

Showta
